{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 7462 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "9e99911c80d4dac9838c0e9fda8d5ca9ac453e78c24a5d12ec909a7c790560f7" :edb/db-before-t 7461 :edb/db-after-t 7462 :edb/tx-data [[13194139540774 50 #inst "2026-10-25T22:59:32.876Z" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186045416 1117 #edb/ref 17592186702638 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186045416 1117 #edb/ref 17592186702663 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186045416 1118 #edb/ref 17592186049061 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186045416 1118 #edb/ref 17592186049606 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186045416 1118 #edb/ref 17592186049617 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186045416 1118 #edb/ref 17592186049685 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186045416 1118 #edb/ref 17592186050104 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186045416 1118 #edb/ref 17592186050773 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186045416 1118 #edb/ref 17592186050992 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702471 1080 "63" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702471 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702471 1083 #uuid "001f6717-2818-512e-be5a-c55f0d4a02ee" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702472 1080 "Both methods give $2\\pi$. The field is defined and continuously differentiable on and inside the unit circle, so Green's theorem legitimately relates the two integrals." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702472 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702472 1083 #uuid "01897633-62d1-5651-8be2-8c9be4da1364" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702473 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049222 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702473 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050426 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702473 1089 #edb/ref 17592186702562 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702473 1090 #uuid "028d712a-b912-5ef3-9ee9-6f63a29a9c6e" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702474 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702554 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702474 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702474 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702474 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702554 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702474 1079 #uuid "03ef442c-08c2-5fcb-b489-c016fa035c1a" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702475 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702599 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702475 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702475 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702475 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702599 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702475 1079 #uuid "04e2bfc2-14e3-57f6-9455-b8939e0b7a26" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702476 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049084 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702476 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050560 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702476 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050773 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702476 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050992 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702476 1088 #edb/ref 17592186051070 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702476 1088 #edb/ref 17592186051085 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702476 1089 #edb/ref 17592186702702 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702476 1090 #uuid "050d3600-ae8b-5b5e-a899-d578a8db32f0" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702477 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702575 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702477 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702477 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702477 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702575 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702477 1079 #uuid "07f2f829-22f9-542b-9ade-2169285ea987" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702478 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702589 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702478 1071 "**(b)(ii)** For $\\mathbf F=\\left\\langle\\frac{x}{x^2+y^2},\\frac{y}{x^2+y^2}\\right\\rangle$, calculate $\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds$ **by hand**. Show your parametrization and work.\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702478 1072 #uuid "08ca2ee1-3c85-5376-b8cd-192882ba0fb1" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702478 1074 "On the unit circle, parametrize by $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. The outward unit normal is $(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ and $ds=dt$. Each field in this problem restricts to $(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ on $C$, so the flux is $\\int_0^{2\\pi}(\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t)\\,dt=2\\pi$. Equivalently, rotate the field as in Problem 1(c) and use the corresponding circulation in Problem 2." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702479 1080 "Parametrize the counterclockwise curve by $\\mathbf r(t)=(x(t),y(t))$. Where the tangent exists, the outward normal is the clockwise rotation of the tangent, hence $\\mathbf n\\,ds=(y'(t),-x'(t))\\,dt$. Therefore\n\n$$\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds=(fy'-gx')\\,dt=(-g,f)\\cdot(x',y')\\,dt=\\mathbf G\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds.$$\n\nIntegrate this pointwise identity over $C$ (piecewise for a piecewise smooth curve)." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702479 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702479 1083 #uuid "0c023af7-0fa4-5186-8428-fbbabdcb5778" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702480 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702682 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702480 1071 "**(c)** Show that\n\n$$\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds=\\oint_C\\mathbf G\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds.$$\n\n**Source note:** Green's theorem may not apply. Instead, try to show $\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds=\\mathbf G\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds$." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702480 1072 #uuid "0c4ee11a-932b-509f-9464-bca3b0b4f4af" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702480 1074 "The equality depends on the orientation and the quarter-turn relating the two fields. It requires the boundary integrals to exist, but does not require either field to be continuously differentiable throughout the interior, so it remains useful when Green's theorem is unavailable." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702481 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049061 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702481 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049470 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702481 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049511 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702481 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049617 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702481 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050031 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702481 1089 #edb/ref 17592186702676 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702481 1090 #uuid "10402c47-9a5a-538d-911f-3aa0c2bdb22a" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702482 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702525 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702482 1071 "**(c)(iv)** For $\\mathbf F=\\left\\langle\\frac{-y}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}},\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\\right\\rangle$, compare your answers to (ii) and (iii). Use the domain from (i) to explain any difference." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702482 1072 #uuid "14788faa-d3cc-5182-ad0e-db1af58157e7" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702482 1074 "The domain and interior regularity explain whether the usual theorem applies. A matching numerical value can also occur through a justified improper limit." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702483 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702629 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702483 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702483 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702483 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702629 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702483 1079 #uuid "152a12f0-6d22-58b0-8680-bd6807c31650" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702484 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702585 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702484 1071 "**(b)(iii)** For $\\mathbf F=\\left\\langle\\frac{-y}{x^2+y^2},\\frac{x}{x^2+y^2}\\right\\rangle$, calculate $\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds$ “using” Green's theorem. Address any failure of its hypotheses." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702484 1072 #uuid "1702368e-f0bc-528a-a104-7f0612080884" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702484 1074 "The formal value $0$ is the result the question asks you to compare, not a correct value of the contour integral. On an annulus $\\varepsilon\\le r\\le1$, Green's theorem has both outer and inner boundaries. The outer integral minus the counterclockwise inner integral is $0$; each counterclockwise integral is $2\\pi$. Omitting the inner boundary loses that contribution." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702485 1080 "\\mathbb R^2\\setminus\\{(0,0)\\}" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702485 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702485 1083 #uuid "173df41c-e05b-574e-aa20-a8d310566036" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702486 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702631 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702486 1071 "**(b)** Show that $\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf G$. In particular, show $\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=0$ iff $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf G=0$. Conclude that $\\mathbf F$ is source-free iff $\\mathbf G$ is irrotational." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702486 1072 #uuid "181efbc3-58c9-5d77-9233-3e50034e40ce" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702486 1074 "For $\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, divergence is $P_x+Q_y$, while planar curl is $Q_x-P_y$. The minus sign on the first component of $\\mathbf G$ makes its curl equal to the divergence of $\\mathbf F$." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702487 1084 #edb/ref 17592186702504 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702487 1084 #edb/ref 17592186702558 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702487 1084 #edb/ref 17592186702639 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702487 1085 "Source: Problem 4(a–c), PDF page 5.\n\n**Ideal Fluids.** The source describes an ideal fluid as one with “zero viscosity (zero divergence)” and “no vortex (zero curl).” Use its stated mathematical conditions of zero divergence and zero curl for this problem. For each pair, complete (i) the conservativeness and ideal-flow discussion and (ii) the potential/stream-function construction." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702487 1086 #uuid "18efe2aa-0ee7-5577-80d1-bacd43921887" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702487 1087 #edb/ref 17592186702639 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702488 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702526 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702488 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702488 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702488 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702526 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702488 1079 #uuid "1b5e55df-c0dd-51ea-8df7-d165c685c016" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702489 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702664 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702489 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702489 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702489 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702664 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702489 1079 #uuid "1ca929f9-9e00-5d43-8750-3ee995575df3" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702490 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702506 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702490 1071 "Source exercise: 3.1.24, printed page **129** (PDF page 141); the assignment sheet lists page 128.\n\nFind the determinant, **using row operations to simplify first**. Show your row operations and track their effects on the determinant.\n\n$$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1&2&1&2\\\\3&1&-2&3\\\\-1&0&3&1\\\\2&3&2&-2\\end{bmatrix}.$$\n\n$\\det(A)=$ {{answer}}." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702490 1072 #uuid "1fb3669f-d6c2-5778-940b-a11e36dc9cd0" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702490 1074 "First use $R_2\\leftarrow R_2-3R_1$, $R_3\\leftarrow R_3+R_1$, and $R_4\\leftarrow R_4-2R_1$. These row replacements preserve the determinant:\n\n$$\\begin{bmatrix}1&2&1&2\\\\0&-5&-5&-3\\\\0&2&4&3\\\\0&-1&0&-6\\end{bmatrix}.$$\n\nSwap $R_2$ and $R_4$ once, changing the determinant's sign. Then apply $R_3\\leftarrow R_3+2R_2$ and $R_4\\leftarrow R_4-5R_2$:\n\n$$\\begin{bmatrix}1&2&1&2\\\\0&-1&0&-6\\\\0&0&4&-9\\\\0&0&-5&27\\end{bmatrix}.$$\n\nFinally, $R_4\\leftarrow R_4+\\tfrac54R_3$ gives upper triangular diagonal entries $1,-1,4,63/4$. Its determinant is $-63$. There was one row swap, so $\\det(A)=63$." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702491 1080 "\\mathbb R^2" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702491 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702491 1083 #uuid "21410ab2-1320-5733-a152-ba355682038b" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702492 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048146 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702492 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049044 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702492 1089 #edb/ref 17592186702658 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702492 1090 #uuid "241acdb1-6385-5094-8074-6720c916f1c8" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702493 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702646 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702493 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702493 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702493 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702646 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702493 1079 #uuid "2520bb94-4364-510b-ad49-85cc3ab85797" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702494 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702524 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702494 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702494 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702494 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702524 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702494 1079 #uuid "255fb13b-2536-5f76-84c6-25f80be7d7c1" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702495 1080 "2\\pi" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702495 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702495 1083 #uuid "278bcbd6-88ef-53b1-8c5c-f9a389d47fa5" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702496 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702477 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702496 1071 "**(c)(ii)** For $\\mathbf F=\\left\\langle\\frac{-y}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}},\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\\right\\rangle$, calculate $\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds$ **by hand**. Show your parametrization and work.\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702496 1072 #uuid "2913247e-6fe4-5e09-8a9d-8346095c1d59" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702496 1074 "Use $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. On the unit circle the denominator, when present, equals $1$, so $\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))=(-\\sin t,\\cos t)=\\mathbf r'(t)$. Thus $\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)\\,dt=dt$ and the integral is $\\int_0^{2\\pi}1\\,dt=2\\pi$." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702497 1064 "**MTH 341 — Written Homework 4**\n\nDue: 2026-10-28\nDue time: 11:59 p.m. PDT\nDeadline source: [Fall 2026 Canvas assignment](https://canvas.oregonstate.edu/courses/2064363/assignments/10695097), checked 2026-10-25 (Wednesday, October 28).\nDate imported: 2026-10-25\nSource: [MTH341-WHW4.pdf](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FWHW-4%2FMTH341-WHW4.pdf)\nTextbook: [A First Course in Linear Algebra, Version 2017 — Revision A](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf), Lyryx Learning, based on K. Kuttler (CC BY).\n\nStudy answers and worked explanations are independently authored additions. The current deadline comes from Fall 2026 Canvas; historical Spring dates extracted from the assignment PDF do not determine this deadline. Textbook exercise identifiers resolve two inaccurate page references on the assignment sheet.\n\n## Instructions\n\n- Complete all 13 assigned exercises, including all parts of Exercise 3.1.1 and only part (b) of Exercise 3.1.3.\n- Calculate Problems 1 and 2 **by hand, without technology**. Technology may be used to row reduce matrices elsewhere unless the problem specifies a method. Problem 8 specifically requires row operations to simplify the determinant first.\n- Show all work and write neatly. Put solutions on separate 8.5-by-11-inch or A4 sheets, using the assignment sheet as the cover. Canvas also allows handwritten work scanned to PDF or typeset work. Submit one PDF to Canvas by the deadline; late homework is not accepted.\n- The assignment points to the definition of a linear combination on textbook pages 30 and 151, and to Week 2 lecture Problems 6–7 for §§1.2.3–1.2.4. A linear combination of vectors is a sum of scalar multiples of those vectors.\n- The Canvas rubric assigns 8 points for completion and 2 points for correctly solving Problem 4 (Exercise 3.1.12)." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702497 1066 #uuid "29d2d464-a28f-51b4-b672-b6fa1126d86f" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702497 1067 "Assignment information" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702498 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702680 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702498 1071 "**(a)(iv)** For $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,y\\rangle$, compare your answers to (ii) and (iii). Use the domain from (i) to explain any difference." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702498 1072 #uuid "2a25b401-c73c-5a57-b5f2-0d4b32e70920" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702498 1074 "The domain and interior regularity explain whether the usual theorem applies. Account for every boundary component when working on a punctured region." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702499 1084 #edb/ref 17592186702559 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702499 1084 #edb/ref 17592186702561 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702499 1084 #edb/ref 17592186702687 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702499 1084 #edb/ref 17592186702696 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702499 1085 "Source: Problem 1(a–d), PDF page 2.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F=\\langle f,g\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf G=\\langle-g,f\\rangle$, and let $C$ be any counterclockwise simple closed curve in the plane." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702499 1086 #uuid "2a27ae01-32f5-5430-8969-0add91b1daf5" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702499 1087 #edb/ref 17592186702559 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702500 1080 "Using planar scalar curl,\n\n$$\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=f_x+g_y,\\qquad\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf G=f_x-(-g)_y=f_x+g_y.$$\n\nThe expressions are identical, so one is zero exactly when the other is zero. These are the definitions of source-free and irrotational, respectively." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702500 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702500 1083 #uuid "2a77ad0c-9822-5e2c-b80f-2602ffd6d7aa" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702501 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702666 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702501 1062 #uuid "2af56ba8-6bc0-5bd1-9494-bf154ab4822f" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702501 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702574 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702502 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702668 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702502 1071 "**(d)** Show that $\\zeta$ is a potential function for $\\mathbf G$ iff it is a stream function for $\\mathbf F$." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702502 1072 #uuid "2b06432f-f231-55ae-8a34-e4d79f103439" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702502 1074 "Check both partial derivatives and their signs. This equivalence concerns a function defined on the specified domain; local potentials do not automatically give a single global stream function on a domain with a hole." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702503 1080 "The instruction that both fields are conservative is false on the full stated domain. A global potential for $\\mathbf F$ is\n\n$$\\phi_F=\\tfrac12\\ln(x^2+y^2)=\\ln r.$$\n\nHowever, $\\oint_{r=1}\\mathbf G\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=2\\pi\\ne0$, so $\\mathbf G$ has no single-valued global potential on the punctured plane. Consequently $\\mathbf F$ has no single-valued global stream function there.\n\nLocally, after choosing a continuous angle branch $\\theta=\\arg(x+iy)$ on a cut domain, $\\nabla\\theta=\\mathbf G$. Thus $\\phi_G=\\theta$ and $\\psi_F=\\theta$ work locally; on $x>0$ one may use $\\theta=\\arctan(y/x)$.\n\nBoth fields nevertheless have zero divergence and zero curl at every point of the stated domain. With $s=x^2+y^2$,\n\n$$\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=\\frac{s-2x^2+s-2y^2}{s^2}=0,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F=\\frac{-2xy+2xy}{s^2}=0,$$\n\n$$\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf G=\\frac{2xy-2xy}{s^2}=0,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf G=\\frac{s-2x^2+s-2y^2}{s^2}=0.$$\n\nSo both meet the worksheet's zero-divergence/zero-curl conditions without both being globally conservative." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702503 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702503 1083 #uuid "2b0dd9f7-e858-58c2-9622-30b01c571ba7" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702504 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702558 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702504 1062 #uuid "2b5d7638-470c-5cb9-a45c-f5e921054684" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702504 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702619 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702505 1080 "A potential for $\\mathbf G=\\langle-g,f\\rangle$ satisfies $\\zeta_x=-g$ and $\\zeta_y=f$. The definition of a stream function for $\\mathbf F=\\langle f,g\\rangle$ is precisely $\\zeta_y=f$, $\\zeta_x=-g$. The two conditions are identical in both directions." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702505 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702505 1083 #uuid "2b63134e-55f3-5061-8a0c-a841870a4892" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702506 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702471 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702506 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702506 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702506 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702471 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702506 1079 #uuid "2b9aeba5-da06-5e9b-a4e0-532626735baf" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702507 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702520 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702507 1062 #uuid "2e6539a3-a450-526d-8ab4-f9917718b78b" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702507 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702478 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702508 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702543 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702508 1062 #uuid "2e731cbf-effb-54cf-ae8f-3968e42893e9" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702508 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702521 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702509 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702517 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702509 1071 "**(c)** Use (a) and (b) to prove the source's statement that “$\\psi$ is everywhere tangent to $\\mathbf F$.” That is, show that the **level curves** of a stream function are streamlines." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702509 1072 #uuid "2f3d1841-9934-5d9b-8ff9-00e6d5262f9c" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702509 1074 "It is the level curve, rather than the scalar function itself, that has a tangent. The slope proof needs $f\\ne0$; the gradient argument handles vertical tangents. A claim of a uniquely defined tangent at every critical point would need additional hypotheses, so retain the regular-point qualification." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702510 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702674 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702510 1062 #uuid "2fbb8807-5f05-53de-9b4c-a755251602c5" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702510 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702496 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702511 1080 "2" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702511 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702511 1083 #uuid "31371801-c4b1-5108-988b-f1287d84a642" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702512 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702661 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702512 1071 "Source exercise: 3.1.12, printed page 128 (PDF page 140).\n\nIdentify the operation that changes the first matrix to the second, and explain its effect on the determinant:\n\n$$\\begin{bmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{bmatrix}\\longrightarrow\\cdots\\longrightarrow\\begin{bmatrix}a&b\\\\2c&2d\\end{bmatrix}.$$" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702512 1072 #uuid "31437968-eb0b-5e95-8502-b20a3264c863" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702512 1074 "Scaling one row by $2$ scales the determinant by $2$. Directly, $a(2d)-b(2c)=2(ad-bc)$. Only one row is scaled, so the factor is $2$, rather than $2^2$." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702513 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702555 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702513 1062 #uuid "32b089e4-8c50-5f23-9ff8-17e91aec80d5" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702513 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702610 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702514 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702653 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702514 1071 "Source exercise: 3.1.20, printed page 129 (PDF page 141).\n\nLet $A,B$ be $n\\times n$ matrices. The notation $A\\sim B$ (**$A$ is similar to $B$**) means that some invertible matrix $P$ satisfies $A=P^{-1}BP$.\n\nShow that $A\\sim B$ implies $\\det(A)=\\det(B)$." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702514 1072 #uuid "33ae2fc2-38de-584c-a278-a991ff186cec" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702514 1074 "Use $\\det(XY)=\\det(X)\\det(Y)$ and $\\det(P^{-1})=1/\\det(P)$. Similarity uses the same invertible $P$ on the two sides of $B$ with opposite powers. The notation $\\sim$ here means similarity, as defined in this exercise." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702515 1080 "By multiplicativity,\n\n$$\\det(A)=\\det(P^{-1}BP)=\\det(P^{-1})\\det(B)\\det(P)\n=\\frac{1}{\\det(P)}\\det(B)\\det(P)=\\det(B).$$\n\nThe cancellation is valid because $P$ is invertible, so $\\det(P)\\ne0$." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702515 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702515 1083 #uuid "33bdc090-a755-588f-843a-154b39670898" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702516 1064 "**MTH 255 — Written Homework 5**\n\nDue: 2026-10-29\nDue time: 11:59 p.m. PDT\nDeadline source: [Fall 2026 Canvas assignment](https://canvas.oregonstate.edu/courses/2064132/assignments/10705976), checked 2026-10-25 (Thursday, October 29).\nDate imported: 2026-10-25\nSource: [MTH255-WHW-Week5_v1.pdf](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FWHW-5%2FMTH255-WHW-Week5_v1.pdf)\n\nStudy answers, worked explanations, and corrections discussed in the reveal fields are independently authored additions. The original tasks are retained, including assertions and notation that require qualification.\n\n## Instructions\n\n- Complete all five problems and their labeled subparts. Show clear, neat supporting work or a written explanation where appropriate. Correct answers without supporting work do not earn full credit.\n- Work on the printed template, write/type on the PDF with a tablet, or use your own paper with the same layout.\n- Submit a single PDF to Gradescope under **Written Homework 5**, in the template's six-page format: the cover page followed by one page for each problem. Include the cover and blank problems. If a solution uses multiple physical pages, combine them into one collage page for that problem. A scan of the printed template should preserve its page count and format.\n- The source awards partial credit for logical, organized progress. Canvas states that late homework is not accepted; submit completed work by the deadline.\n- In Problems 1–3, $\\mathbf T$ is the unit tangent in the stated counterclockwise direction and $\\mathbf n$ is the outward unit normal. Preserve the distinction between evaluating a contour integral directly and checking whether Green's theorem applies." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702516 1066 #uuid "39bc60ca-a0c0-539d-aa68-d26bd190f094" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702516 1067 "Assignment information" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702517 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702522 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702517 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702517 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702517 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702522 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702517 1079 #uuid "39cb848b-3dc7-55a4-b390-6f24001982e0" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702518 1080 "At $P=(x_0,y_0)$, parametrize the line by $(x,y)=(x_0,y_0)+t(f(x_0,y_0),g(x_0,y_0))$. Its slope is $\\Delta y/\\Delta x=g(x_0,y_0)/f(x_0,y_0)$ when $f(x_0,y_0)\\ne0$. If $f=0$ and $g\\ne0$, the line is vertical. If $f=g=0$, the zero field vector specifies no direction." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702518 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702518 1083 #uuid "3e9ebcbc-b2da-58eb-b0aa-d994e87d860f" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702519 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049159 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702519 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049875 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702519 1089 #edb/ref 17592186702514 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702519 1090 #uuid "3f7f8086-c6d9-5b75-8752-694717e6ed09" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702520 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702637 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702520 1062 #uuid "4083b77e-cc57-514b-8d88-9590973c581e" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702520 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702692 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702521 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702641 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702521 1071 "**(b)** Use implicit differentiation of $\\psi(x,y)=c$ to show the displayed relation in the source:\n\n$$\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{\\psi_x}{\\psi_t}.$$\n\nThe denominator $\\psi_t$ is retained **as printed** in the PDF. Address the notation when deriving the relation." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702521 1072 #uuid "43a7a084-6042-5631-9a50-f49ed6051e5f" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702521 1074 "Apply the chain rule to both dependencies of $\\psi$: its explicit $x$ argument and its $y(x)$ argument. No variable $t$ was introduced in the source context. Division by $\\psi_y$ requires $\\psi_y\\ne0$; other regular points can instead be described locally by $x$ as a function of $y$." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702522 1080 "At a point with $f\\ne0$, substitute $\\psi_x=-g$ and $\\psi_y=f$ into the corrected relation from (b):\n\n$$\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{\\psi_x}{\\psi_y}=\\frac{g}{f}.$$\n\nThis is the slope of the field direction from (a), so the level curve is tangent to $\\mathbf F$.\n\nMore generally,\n\n$$\\nabla\\psi\\cdot\\mathbf F=(-g,f)\\cdot(f,g)=0.$$\n\nAt a regular point where $\\nabla\\psi\\ne0$, the gradient is normal to the level curve, so $\\mathbf F$ is tangent to it, including vertical tangents. Thus regular level curves follow the flow. At a stagnation point $\\mathbf F=0$, no nonzero field direction is specified; the zero vector still satisfies the orthogonality identity." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702522 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702522 1083 #uuid "447c4af3-2141-5ebb-8aad-46e4298a2833" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702523 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702628 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702523 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702523 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702523 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702628 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702523 1079 #uuid "454c7bd9-c191-5f51-b426-646010d75d0f" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702524 1080 "Both computations yield $2\\pi$, but the agreement alone does not make a direct full-disk invocation of Green's theorem valid. The origin is excluded from the domain. In this case, annular applications and a limit do justify the equality, because the inner-boundary contribution tends to zero." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702524 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702524 1083 #uuid "462deff5-b92c-575b-bde8-d2e265a1bc6f" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702525 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702530 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702525 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702525 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702525 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702530 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702525 1079 #uuid "478334df-2d59-5e3d-884a-48ed7220e653" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702526 1080 "The matrix is transposed. Transposing a square matrix preserves its determinant: $\\det(A^T)=\\det(A)$." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702526 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702526 1083 #uuid "4a7c0a2b-3a9f-5741-ad2d-ee14622e3578" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702527 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702693 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702527 1062 #uuid "4b721ba3-1505-5c29-b49e-adbc03da8df1" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702527 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702600 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702528 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702587 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702528 1062 #uuid "4c7f0d05-07b4-5c2a-9464-48058549eacb" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702528 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702672 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702529 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702501 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702529 1062 #uuid "4d32eadb-a99d-5877-a2b9-3d6834c2c7de" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702529 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702516 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702530 1080 "Both computations yield $2\\pi$, but the agreement alone does not make a direct full-disk invocation of Green's theorem valid. The origin is excluded from the domain. In this case, annular applications and a limit do justify the equality, because the inner-boundary contribution tends to zero." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702530 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702530 1083 #uuid "4e6eb92b-c2bf-5fd9-ac1d-05691eb1508e" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702531 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702694 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702531 1062 #uuid "505503e2-e0a9-5cf7-9275-50e547bddb8e" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702531 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702617 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702532 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702652 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702532 1071 "**(b)** Find the determinant of $\\begin{bmatrix}0&3\\\\0&2\\end{bmatrix}$ **by hand, without technology**. Show your calculation.\n\nDeterminant: {{answer}}." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702532 1072 #uuid "50f6ec1f-a625-56fd-af37-7153d11ff18b" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702532 1074 "$0(2)-3(0)=0$. For a $2\\times2$ matrix, $\\det\\begin{bmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{bmatrix}=ad-bc$. Apply the subtraction in that order." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702533 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702595 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702533 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702533 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702533 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702595 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702533 1079 #uuid "51c81fbb-ed83-50be-ab54-2da572103db1" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702534 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702495 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702534 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702534 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702534 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702495 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702534 1079 #uuid "52d06fdc-3175-52f3-b479-8c57eb944433" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702535 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702604 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702535 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702535 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702535 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702604 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702535 1079 #uuid "53586b83-f875-5cd5-a67c-0ff2bafda146" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702536 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049159 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702536 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050727 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702536 1089 #edb/ref 17592186702577 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702536 1090 #uuid "53997f21-b6f5-5e8a-910e-73f060676eea" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702537 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702640 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702537 1071 "**(b)(i)** For $\\mathbf F=\\left\\langle\\frac{x}{x^2+y^2},\\frac{y}{x^2+y^2}\\right\\rangle$, find its domain.\n\nDomain: {{answer}}." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702537 1072 #uuid "57e7dd59-de67-5fd1-b82e-9bd7d7ff64e8" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702537 1074 "The denominator vanishes exactly at $(0,0)$. Exclude the origin; the field is smooth everywhere else." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702538 1080 "Here $\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=2$. Green's theorem applies because the field is $C^1$ on the entire disk and a neighborhood of it. Therefore\n\n$$\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds=\\iint_D2\\,dA=2\\operatorname{area}(D)=2\\pi.$$" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702538 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702538 1083 #uuid "5805a00c-2392-5a80-91e5-b6d845f1b4dc" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702539 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702528 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702539 1062 #uuid "59a06cff-5ef4-5fd2-b9fc-70bdea66f345" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702539 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702675 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702540 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702556 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702540 1071 "Source exercise: 3.1.19, printed page 128 (PDF page 140).\n\nA real matrix is **orthogonal** if $A^T A=I$, so its inverse is its transpose. What are the possible values of $\\det(A)$?\n\nEnter the set of possible values: {{answer}}." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702540 1072 #uuid "5b075aba-b77c-52f4-9246-23c2d050dae8" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702540 1074 "Taking determinants gives $\\det(A^T)\\det(A)=\\det(I)=1$. Since $\\det(A^T)=\\det(A)$, we have $(\\det A)^2=1$, hence $\\det A\\in\\{-1,1\\}$. Both occur: $I_n$ has determinant $1$, and $\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,\\ldots,1)$ has determinant $-1$." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702541 1080 "Take $\\phi_F=e^x\\cos y$ and $\\phi_G=e^x\\sin y$. Their gradients are exactly the two specified fields, so both are conservative. A stream function for $\\mathbf F$ is $\\psi=e^x\\sin y$.\n\nTheir divergences are $e^x\\cos y-e^x\\cos y=0$ and $e^x\\sin y-e^x\\sin y=0$. Their curls are also zero: for $\\mathbf F$, $-e^x\\sin y-(-e^x\\sin y)=0$; for $\\mathbf G$, $e^x\\cos y-e^x\\cos y=0$. Problem 1(b) gives $\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf G=0$ and, after rotating $\\mathbf G$ to $-\\mathbf F$, $\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf G=-\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F=0$. Thus both meet the stated ideal-flow conditions." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702541 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702541 1083 #uuid "5bf6e3f3-eb49-5df5-bc3e-82d627dcb3d0" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702542 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702699 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702542 1062 #uuid "5c69d8bb-8add-5682-9061-5d47db48f1e5" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702542 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702598 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702543 1062 #uuid "5d6273ec-8db9-5610-a426-8e0e3a559bf4" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702543 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702509 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702544 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702695 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702544 1062 #uuid "61e16448-2f00-556b-9dbc-cb53013d3351" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702544 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702573 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702545 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702485 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702545 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702545 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702545 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702485 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702545 1079 #uuid "624366ae-d5ee-5a3c-832e-2d130d6bef73" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702546 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702659 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702546 1062 #uuid "63d2e57f-e110-5ff4-9ee8-643a619ec0c9" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702546 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702532 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702547 1084 #edb/ref 17592186702546 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702547 1084 #edb/ref 17592186702603 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702547 1084 #edb/ref 17592186702659 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702547 1085 "Source exercise: 3.1.1(a–c), textbook printed page 126 (PDF page 138)." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702547 1086 #uuid "6524fe0f-3387-5099-a4eb-239ad80a370e" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702547 1087 #edb/ref 17592186702603 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702548 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702531 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702548 1062 #uuid "665aecc3-ca3a-51fc-bb3e-eea47d958088" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702548 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702519 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702549 1080 "0" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702549 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702549 1083 #uuid "67f291fb-9f19-59f3-bc4a-4a57c01775f4" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702550 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702608 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702550 1062 #uuid "6931076d-b37a-56f5-a1cb-820094ba9ec2" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702550 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702616 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702551 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702550 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702551 1062 #uuid "6bae0026-dfbe-5639-b8a3-e637e66dd67f" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702551 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702536 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702552 1080 "Yes. The combination is $\\vec v=2\\vec u_1-\\vec u_2$:\n\n$$2\\begin{bmatrix}3\\\\1\\\\-1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2\\\\-2\\\\1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\\\4\\\\-3\\end{bmatrix}.$$" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702552 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702552 1083 #uuid "6df2a8bc-93cf-5744-a847-7101bcc33f96" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702553 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702657 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702553 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702553 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702553 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702657 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702553 1079 #uuid "6f11794d-626e-5b79-9985-1aeb29620714" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702554 1080 "-10" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702554 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702554 1083 #uuid "6f481c20-7669-5276-b056-49ea2dd62d6c" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702555 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702605 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702555 1062 #uuid "6fc68834-3537-544d-9861-179d1dd04245" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702555 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702582 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702556 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702584 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702556 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702556 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702556 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702584 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702556 1079 #uuid "702e7d4b-4c4d-50e8-8cd7-8bd6c18744bf" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702557 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702625 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702557 1062 #uuid "7056f59b-ad63-5024-a35d-628649b30b2a" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702557 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702576 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702558 1062 #uuid "7057a4dc-0dc5-54fc-92ab-54e3dd9d1119" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702558 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702594 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702559 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702561 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702559 1062 #uuid "712b84d0-c39b-536e-9e15-461ffda747fb" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702559 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702593 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702560 1080 "Potentials are $\\phi_F=(x^2-y^2)/2$ and $\\phi_G=xy$, since $\\nabla\\phi_F=(x,-y)$ and $\\nabla\\phi_G=(y,x)$. A stream function for $\\mathbf F$ is $\\psi=xy$.\n\nBoth curls are zero because the fields are gradients. Also, $\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=1-1=0$ and $\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf G=0+0=0$. Thus both have zero divergence and zero curl, as required here. Problem 1(b) gives $\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf G=0$. Applying it to $\\mathbf G$, whose rotated field is $-\\mathbf F$, also gives $\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf G=-\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F=0$. Problem 1(d) identifies the potential of $\\mathbf G$ with the stream function of $\\mathbf F$." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702560 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702560 1083 #uuid "71c153c5-b242-591a-bc0e-031852d164f8" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702561 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702696 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702561 1062 #uuid "72d79f5d-9597-59be-baca-aed7d0058847" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702561 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702486 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702562 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702553 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702562 1071 "Source exercise: 4.10.11, printed page 222 (PDF page 234).\n\nAre the following vectors linearly independent? If they are, explain why. If they are not, exhibit one as a linear combination of the others. Also give a linearly independent set with the same span.\n\n$$\\vec v_1=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\3\\\\-1\\\\1\\end{bmatrix},\\quad\\vec v_2=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\4\\\\-1\\\\1\\end{bmatrix},\\quad\\vec v_3=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\4\\\\0\\\\1\\end{bmatrix},\\quad\\vec v_4=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\10\\\\2\\\\1\\end{bmatrix}.$$" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702562 1072 #uuid "7455514b-5312-5142-bf16-0346031c446d" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702562 1074 "The displayed combination checks in every coordinate: $-6+4+3=1$, $-18+16+12=10$, $6-4=2$, and $-6+4+3=1$. The first three rows of the matrix with columns $\\vec v_1,\\vec v_2,\\vec v_3$ form a $3\\times3$ minor with determinant $1$, so these three columns are independent. Removing $\\vec v_4$, which is generated by them, preserves the span." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702563 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049044 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702563 1089 #edb/ref 17592186702547 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702563 1090 #uuid "74b6c0a7-c31c-51a5-928d-fa2353a737e0" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702564 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702691 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702564 1071 "Source exercise: 3.1.17, printed page 128 (PDF page 140).\n\nIs it true that $\\det(A+B)=\\det(A)+\\det(B)$? If true, explain why; if false, give a counterexample." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702564 1072 #uuid "75171c23-6bf6-5fb3-b41d-ec62488dadcc" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702564 1074 "A single pair of square matrices for which the two sides differ disproves the assertion. Here $A+B=\\begin{bmatrix}2&0\\\\0&2\\end{bmatrix}$, whose determinant is $2\\cdot2=4$. The determinant is linear in one row at a time with the other rows fixed, not additive in the whole matrix." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702565 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702635 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702565 1062 #uuid "756c889b-72a9-5f44-b8e9-afe6c2f3e131" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702565 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702634 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702566 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702654 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702566 1062 #uuid "779b5c44-c05a-5a82-a250-0c0e025c01b1" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702566 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702497 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702567 1080 "Direct calculation gives $2\\pi$, while the invalid full-disk use of Green's theorem gives $0$. The domain excludes the origin, so the theorem's regularity hypothesis fails inside $C$. The mismatch comes from ignoring the hole and its inner boundary, rather than from an error in the direct parametrization." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702567 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702567 1083 #uuid "77b2897b-cbba-5228-9728-60335f0049bf" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702568 1080 "Choose every coefficient to be zero. Then $0\\vec x_1+\\cdots+0\\vec x_k=\\vec0$, which is a linear combination of the given vectors. Therefore $\\vec0$ belongs to their span." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702568 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702568 1083 #uuid "77f3dd9d-1119-51df-b553-4a43c4537b8d" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702569 1084 #edb/ref 17592186702507 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702569 1084 #edb/ref 17592186702520 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702569 1084 #edb/ref 17592186702528 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702569 1084 #edb/ref 17592186702539 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702569 1084 #edb/ref 17592186702542 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702569 1084 #edb/ref 17592186702587 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702569 1084 #edb/ref 17592186702611 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702569 1084 #edb/ref 17592186702612 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702569 1084 #edb/ref 17592186702613 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702569 1084 #edb/ref 17592186702637 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702569 1084 #edb/ref 17592186702648 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702569 1084 #edb/ref 17592186702699 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702569 1085 "Source: Problem 3(a–c), each with tasks (i–iv), PDF page 4.\n\nLet $C$ be the counterclockwise unit circle centered at the origin. For each field in parts (a), (b), and (c): (i) find its domain; (ii) calculate the indicated integral by hand; (iii) calculate it “using” Green's theorem; and (iv) compare (ii) and (iii), using (i) to explain any difference.\n\n**Source hint:** Use the previous two problems to avoid additional calculations." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702569 1086 #uuid "790bf7b9-b5d7-5639-b5b0-fe2cef244b42" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702569 1087 #edb/ref 17592186702613 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702570 1080 "2\\pi" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702570 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702570 1083 #uuid "790d672e-a0a4-5642-aed9-0114d854a533" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702571 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702518 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702571 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702571 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702571 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702518 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702571 1079 #uuid "7a5da541-73c6-5b07-965c-962d03521a2f" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702572 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702523 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702572 1071 "**(a)(i)** For $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,y\\rangle$, find its domain.\n\nDomain: {{answer}}." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702572 1072 #uuid "7c429fff-d597-5eb7-b988-201b2590aeda" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702572 1074 "The components are polynomial functions, so they are defined for every real pair $(x,y)$." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702573 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702579 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702573 1071 "**(a)(iii)** For $\\mathbf F=\\langle-y,x\\rangle$, calculate $\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds$ “using” Green's theorem. Address any failure of its hypotheses." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702573 1072 #uuid "7cbfe38b-fd98-546b-9207-85c535713b0c" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702573 1074 "Check the theorem hypotheses before replacing the boundary integral with an area integral. This field has no singularity in the disk." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702574 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048449 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702574 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048630 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702574 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049459 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702574 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049617 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702574 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049853 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702574 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050104 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702574 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050793 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702574 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050983 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702574 1088 #edb/ref 17592186051085 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702574 1089 #edb/ref 17592186702499 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702574 1090 #uuid "7d4694a1-9fe8-5673-83c5-cfe56487b8d2" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702575 1080 "2\\pi" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702575 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702575 1083 #uuid "7f2ad2d7-d16c-56f6-871b-e9c3cb1c2424" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702618 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702576 1071 "**(a)(i)** For $\\mathbf F=\\langle-y,x\\rangle$, find its domain.\n\nDomain: {{answer}}." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702576 1072 #uuid "801c49f8-6d3c-5b5d-bb45-b51699b6a588" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702576 1074 "The components are polynomial functions, so they are defined for every real pair $(x,y)$." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702577 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702488 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702577 1071 "Source exercise: 3.1.9, printed page **127** (PDF page 139); the assignment sheet lists page 128.\n\nIdentify the operation that changes the first matrix to the second, and explain its effect on the determinant:\n\n$$\\begin{bmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{bmatrix}\\longrightarrow\\cdots\\longrightarrow\\begin{bmatrix}a&c\\\\b&d\\end{bmatrix}.$$" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702577 1072 #uuid "805d0579-8e06-52e1-98ec-6d81588ca504" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702577 1074 "The second matrix has the old rows as columns. Directly, the first determinant is $ad-bc$, and the second is $ad-cb=ad-bc$. This is transposition, rather than swapping two rows." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702578 1062 #uuid "81d64fff-0bc5-5d3e-830b-e7c9b7dd2f5f" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702578 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702482 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702579 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702642 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702579 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702579 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702579 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702642 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702579 1079 #uuid "824eb4db-8c50-5363-bae5-8bcf33c2c819" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702580 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702636 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702580 1071 "Source exercise: 4.10.10, printed page 222 (PDF page 234).\n\nSuppose $\\{\\vec x_1,\\ldots,\\vec x_k\\}$ is a set of vectors in $\\mathbb R^n$. Show that $\\vec0$ is in $\\operatorname{span}\\{\\vec x_1,\\ldots,\\vec x_k\\}$." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702580 1072 #uuid "83017630-13a4-55f0-8afa-98393d3821c9" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702580 1074 "A span allows arbitrary real coefficients, including all zeros. The argument does not require the vectors to be nonzero or independent. Producing zero with all-zero coefficients does not demonstrate dependence; dependence requires a nontrivial zero combination." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702620 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702581 1071 "**(a)(ii)** For $\\mathbf F=\\langle-y,x\\rangle$, calculate $\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds$ **by hand**. Show your parametrization and work.\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702581 1072 #uuid "8391dbe2-9a3e-510f-9e77-9168461afd0d" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702581 1074 "Use $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. On the unit circle the denominator, when present, equals $1$, so $\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))=(-\\sin t,\\cos t)=\\mathbf r'(t)$. Thus $\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)\\,dt=dt$ and the integral is $\\int_0^{2\\pi}1\\,dt=2\\pi$." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702535 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702582 1071 "**(b)(ii)** For $\\mathbf F=\\left\\langle\\frac{-y}{x^2+y^2},\\frac{x}{x^2+y^2}\\right\\rangle$, calculate $\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds$ **by hand**. Show your parametrization and work.\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702582 1072 #uuid "83b569b0-0b6b-5273-ae4e-6e0b127fd30d" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702582 1074 "Use $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. On the unit circle the denominator, when present, equals $1$, so $\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))=(-\\sin t,\\cos t)=\\mathbf r'(t)$. Thus $\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)\\,dt=dt$ and the integral is $\\int_0^{2\\pi}1\\,dt=2\\pi$." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702583 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702615 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702583 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702583 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702583 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702615 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702583 1079 #uuid "83f4bdec-7b5a-5c57-b8ae-f451d3a4a3f2" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702584 1080 "\\{-1,1\\}" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702584 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702584 1083 #uuid "8958b642-4a3f-58ce-9a4b-3091996a4255" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702585 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702622 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702585 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702585 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702585 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702622 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702585 1079 #uuid "8a26eece-73eb-5425-ab5c-008b42b0892b" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702586 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049044 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702586 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049875 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702586 1089 #edb/ref 17592186702564 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702586 1090 #uuid "8ab89569-9d66-5da7-a554-aaf39f6e0697" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702587 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702611 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702587 1062 #uuid "8b2c51cf-e9bb-5169-b3a0-ecf77bcd631f" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702587 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702498 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702588 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702649 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702588 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702588 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702588 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702649 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702588 1079 #uuid "8b628751-9b0a-5e3a-8424-44e093fc265c" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702589 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702700 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702589 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702589 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702589 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702700 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702589 1079 #uuid "8ba64de1-78df-526b-a684-fb466addd1c0" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702590 1080 "They are dependent because $\\vec v_3=-2\\vec v_1+3\\vec v_2$. An independent set with the same span is $\\{\\vec v_1,\\vec v_2,\\vec v_4\\}$." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702590 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702590 1083 #uuid "8d0ab866-196e-52dc-89f3-7c3a4c5069af" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702591 1062 #uuid "8d0b5095-bbc4-5d23-8f02-118c72d10b29" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702591 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702678 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702592 1080 "\\mathbb R^2\\setminus\\{(0,0)\\}" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702592 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702592 1083 #uuid "8d6cdc8a-250d-53ce-88eb-c7c7b3345fb1" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702593 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702597 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702593 1071 "**(a)** Show that $\\mathbf F$ has continuous first derivatives on and inside $C$ if and only if $\\mathbf G$ does." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702593 1072 #uuid "902c743a-020b-59b4-af9d-0ead240033fc" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702593 1074 "Both fields use the same two scalar functions, with a sign change and a component swap. Check continuity of all four first partial derivatives on the same region; no application of Green's theorem is needed." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702594 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702609 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702594 1071 "**(c)** On $\\mathbb R^2\\setminus\\{(0,0)\\}$, let\n\n$$\\mathbf F=\\left\\langle\\frac{x}{x^2+y^2},\\frac{y}{x^2+y^2}\\right\\rangle,\\qquad\n\\mathbf G=\\left\\langle\\frac{-y}{x^2+y^2},\\frac{x}{x^2+y^2}\\right\\rangle.$$\n\n(i) Confirm both are conservative. Use Problem 1 to show $\\mathbf F$ is an ideal flow (and so is $\\mathbf G$).\n\n(ii) Find potential functions for both fields. Use Problem 1 to derive a stream function for $\\mathbf F$." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702594 1072 #uuid "907e212d-1b2f-56e7-8af5-1ad42ba5d639" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702594 1074 "Curl zero on a domain with a hole does not guarantee a global potential. Use Problem 2(b)'s nonzero circulation to disprove the global claim for $\\mathbf G$. A discontinuous principal angle or a multi-valued angle is not a single-valued differentiable potential on the whole punctured plane. Problem 1(d) transfers exactly this obstruction to a global stream function for $\\mathbf F$." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702595 1080 "Both methods give $2\\pi$. The field is defined and continuously differentiable on and inside the unit circle, so Green's theorem legitimately relates the two integrals." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702595 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702595 1083 #uuid "9183a5c7-138b-5916-9785-973488ce8174" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702596 1080 "The second row is multiplied by $2$: $R_2\\leftarrow2R_2$. The new determinant is $2$ times the old determinant." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702596 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702596 1083 #uuid "91b1038e-0a2e-56d6-8c20-76c870460b61" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702597 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702607 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702597 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702597 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702597 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702607 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702597 1079 #uuid "9247c709-6ec6-5fab-9361-a6bbbe35271d" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702598 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702534 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702598 1071 "**(c)(ii)** For $\\mathbf F=\\left\\langle\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}},\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\\right\\rangle$, calculate $\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds$ **by hand**. Show your parametrization and work.\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702598 1072 #uuid "928d7776-a536-5089-a550-fc78255e94dc" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702598 1074 "On the unit circle, parametrize by $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. The outward unit normal is $(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ and $ds=dt$. Each field in this problem restricts to $(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ on $C$, so the flux is $\\int_0^{2\\pi}(\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t)\\,dt=2\\pi$. Equivalently, rotate the field as in Problem 1(c) and use the corresponding circulation in Problem 2." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702599 1080 "For $r>0$, $\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=1/r$. The formal area integral can be interpreted as an improper integral:\n\n$$\\lim_{\\varepsilon\\to0^+}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_\\varepsilon^1\\frac1r\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\n=\\lim_{\\varepsilon\\to0^+}2\\pi(1-\\varepsilon)=2\\pi.$$\n\nThe standard full-disk theorem does not apply directly because the field is undefined at the origin. The equality can be justified by applying Green's theorem to annuli and taking a limit." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702599 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702599 1083 #uuid "92df2acd-bb90-5c44-81fa-525df536f59e" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702600 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048308 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702600 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049459 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702600 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049617 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702600 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050869 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702600 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050983 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702600 1088 #edb/ref 17592186051085 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702600 1089 #edb/ref 17592186702487 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702600 1090 #uuid "9338c8a4-4277-5d41-af4d-ba8938f0d609" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702601 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702551 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702601 1062 #uuid "934db3d5-d06b-5ff6-9c96-79c1f768df3d" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702601 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702492 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702602 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702655 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702602 1071 "Source exercise: 4.10.18, printed page 224 (PDF page 236).\n\nAre the following vectors linearly independent? If they are, explain why. If they are not, exhibit one as a linear combination of the others. Also give a linearly independent set with the same span.\n\n$$\\vec v_1=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\4\\\\-2\\\\1\\end{bmatrix},\\quad\\vec v_2=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\5\\\\-3\\\\1\\end{bmatrix},\\quad\\vec v_3=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\7\\\\-5\\\\1\\end{bmatrix},\\quad\\vec v_4=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\5\\\\-2\\\\1\\end{bmatrix}.$$" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702602 1072 #uuid "966de5ce-896c-5026-ae25-aa55dd1e00a5" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702602 1074 "The combination checks as $-2(1,4,-2,1)+3(1,5,-3,1)=(1,7,-5,1)$. For the proposed independent set, use the first three coordinate rows. The determinant of $\\begin{bmatrix}1&1&1\\\\4&5&5\\\\-2&-3&-2\\end{bmatrix}$ is $1$, proving independence. The removed vector $\\vec v_3$ is generated by the retained ones, so their spans agree." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702603 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702546 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702603 1062 #uuid "980350aa-c6b9-5833-acad-4f4657469d3c" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702603 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702688 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702604 1080 "2\\pi" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702604 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702604 1083 #uuid "9baff282-1ea0-521b-a768-4edc1668a602" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702605 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702565 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702605 1062 #uuid "9bef3c0d-f4cd-5ab3-b23f-494cfc5409d1" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702605 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702484 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702606 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702650 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702606 1071 "**(a)** $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,-y\\rangle$, $\\mathbf G=\\langle y,x\\rangle$ on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n\n(i) Confirm both are conservative. Use Problem 1 to show $\\mathbf F$ is an ideal flow (and so is $\\mathbf G$).\n\n(ii) Find potential functions for both fields. Use Problem 1 to derive a stream function for $\\mathbf F$." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702606 1072 #uuid "9dbe21c8-f881-59e7-ad0d-69a6eb42dd99" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702606 1074 "Differentiate each potential to check both components. For $\\psi=xy$, $\\psi_y=x=F_1$ and $\\psi_x=y=-F_2$. Additive constants do not change any of these fields. The calculations verify the worksheet's stated zero-divergence/zero-curl conditions." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702607 1080 "The first partial derivatives of $\\mathbf F=\\langle f,g\\rangle$ are $f_x,f_y,g_x,g_y$. Those of $\\mathbf G=\\langle-g,f\\rangle$ are $-g_x,-g_y,f_x,f_y$. Negating or reordering functions preserves continuity. Thus all first partial derivatives of one field are continuous on the region exactly when all first partial derivatives of the other are." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702607 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702607 1083 #uuid "9dbe677d-9a8d-5d73-9cab-fff2ee3533b1" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702608 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702643 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702608 1062 #uuid "9df22fd7-2a0d-5dff-935d-d3181fd4416d" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702608 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702586 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702609 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702503 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702609 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702609 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702609 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702503 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702609 1079 #uuid "9e1e9dba-f0f0-566b-bacd-0917f1f61821" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702610 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702689 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702610 1071 "**(b)(i)** For $\\mathbf F=\\left\\langle\\frac{-y}{x^2+y^2},\\frac{x}{x^2+y^2}\\right\\rangle$, find its domain.\n\nDomain: {{answer}}." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702610 1072 #uuid "9ea201a6-ed27-5da6-a639-d9b36c73f06a" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702610 1074 "The denominator vanishes exactly at $(0,0)$. Exclude the origin; the field is smooth everywhere else." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702611 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702507 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702611 1062 #uuid "9ecd13c3-2d0d-5e13-b711-07187cea5b9c" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702611 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702537 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702612 1062 #uuid "9f8d2e27-5f15-58a8-99d0-ddac3e25639e" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702612 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702623 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702613 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702539 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702613 1062 #uuid "9faa75fa-bdb4-5acd-9f12-a59f277b7403" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702613 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702572 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702614 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702475 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702614 1071 "**(c)(iii)** For $\\mathbf F=\\left\\langle\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}},\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\\right\\rangle$, calculate $\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds$ “using” Green's theorem. Address any failure of its hypotheses." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702614 1072 #uuid "9fb2ff51-bb0a-56d9-9343-4ddf27f72f30" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702614 1074 "For a radius-$\\varepsilon$ circle, the counterclockwise circulation (Problem 2) or outward-from-the-small-disk flux (Problem 3) is $2\\pi\\varepsilon$, which tends to zero. The annulus formula is outer integral minus that inner integral $=2\\pi(1-\\varepsilon)$. The Jacobian factor $r$ cancels the singular factor $1/r$ in the improper area integral." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702615 1080 "375" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702615 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702615 1083 #uuid "a0a128c0-135d-59fc-9633-3ad9971ff100" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702616 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048462 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702616 1089 #edb/ref 17592186702512 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702616 1090 #uuid "a219ec3d-cf5a-5302-a36f-6cac1367ef65" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702617 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048462 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702617 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049685 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702617 1089 #edb/ref 17592186702490 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702617 1090 #uuid "a3033eeb-8f3e-53d2-b2aa-d827abb0de9b" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702618 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702491 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702618 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702618 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702618 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702491 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702618 1079 #uuid "a3e230a5-1e46-54cb-a9a4-b1d5b27c32fc" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702619 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702651 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702619 1071 "**(b)** $\\mathbf F=\\langle e^x\\cos y,-e^x\\sin y\\rangle$, $\\mathbf G=\\langle e^x\\sin y,e^x\\cos y\\rangle$ on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n\n(i) Confirm both are conservative. Use Problem 1 to show $\\mathbf F$ is an ideal flow (and so is $\\mathbf G$).\n\n(ii) Find potential functions for both fields. Use Problem 1 to derive a stream function for $\\mathbf F$." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702619 1072 #uuid "a55585d2-0b0b-5446-a66e-5e37d60fbf20" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702619 1074 "For the stream function, check $\\psi_y=e^x\\cos y=F_1$ and $\\psi_x=e^x\\sin y=-F_2$. When differentiating in $y$, the sign in $(\\cos y)'=-\\sin y$ matters. Potentials are defined on the entire stated plane." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702620 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702681 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702620 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702620 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702620 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702681 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702620 1079 #uuid "a64a5703-da96-51f8-bd37-e4a7f704835d" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702621 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702570 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702621 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702621 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702621 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702570 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702621 1079 #uuid "a71b514a-9026-5f64-8d2d-53ce75beba13" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702622 1080 "For $r=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}>0$, $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F=0$. Formally integrating that expression over the punctured disk gives $0$.\n\nThis is not a valid application of Green's theorem to the full disk: the field is undefined at the origin, which lies inside $C$. The actual boundary integral is $2\\pi$." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702622 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702622 1083 #uuid "a7667b11-d5f9-5246-834a-a9f6246331ca" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702623 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702494 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702623 1071 "**(c)(iv)** For $\\mathbf F=\\left\\langle\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}},\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\\right\\rangle$, compare your answers to (ii) and (iii). Use the domain from (i) to explain any difference." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702623 1072 #uuid "a79033ef-5319-5e1b-a2ab-133175d056cc" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702623 1074 "The domain and interior regularity explain whether the usual theorem applies. A matching numerical value can also occur through a justified improper limit." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702624 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049084 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702624 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049459 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702624 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050104 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702624 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050560 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702624 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050694 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702624 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050793 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702624 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050992 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702624 1089 #edb/ref 17592186702569 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702624 1090 #uuid "ad29cf5f-7204-5fcb-b924-237b5f474ab8" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702625 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702544 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702625 1062 #uuid "ae2c7e88-7234-5963-8131-50c9a069b8e8" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702625 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702581 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702626 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702493 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702626 1071 "Source exercise: 4.10.4, printed page 221 (PDF page 233).\n\nConsider\n\n$$\\vec v_1=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\1\\\\-2\\end{bmatrix},\\quad\\vec v_2=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\2\\\\-2\\end{bmatrix},\\quad\\vec v_3=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\-3\\\\-2\\end{bmatrix},\\quad\\vec v_4=\\begin{bmatrix}-1\\\\1\\\\2\\end{bmatrix}.$$\n\nIs $\\vec w=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\2\\\\-1\\end{bmatrix}$ in their span? If so, exhibit a linear combination equal to $\\vec w$, using as few of the four vectors as possible." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702626 1072 #uuid "ae7af27d-2f3d-57f9-859d-20a413d678ca" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702626 1074 "If $\\sum c_j\\vec v_j=\\vec w$, the first-coordinate equation would give $c_1+c_2+c_3-c_4=1$, while the third would give $-2(c_1+c_2+c_3-c_4)=-1$. The first equation makes the third left side $-2$, a contradiction. The requested minimal combination is conditional on membership." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702627 1068 #edb/ref 17592186702701 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702627 1071 "Source exercise: 4.2.3, printed page 151 (PDF page 163).\n\nDecide whether\n\n$$\\vec v=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\\\4\\\\-3\\end{bmatrix}$$\n\nis a linear combination of\n\n$$\\vec u_1=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\\\1\\\\-1\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad\\vec u_2=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\\\-2\\\\1\\end{bmatrix}.$$" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702627 1072 #uuid "aeff0721-b0ec-58e0-9819-86270bef6b68" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702627 1074 "Set $a\\vec u_1+b\\vec u_2=\\vec v$. The first two equations are $3a+2b=4$ and $a-2b=4$, giving $a=2$, $b=-1$. Check the third equation: $-a+b=-2-1=-3$. All three coordinates must agree." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702628 1080 "\\mathbb R^2" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702628 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702628 1083 #uuid "b016c23f-7eca-5170-aed1-5bc2fc4e06bf" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702629 1080 "Direct calculation gives $2\\pi$, while the invalid full-disk use of Green's theorem gives $0$. The domain excludes the origin, so the theorem's regularity hypothesis fails inside $C$. The mismatch comes from ignoring the hole and its inner boundary, rather than from an error in the direct parametrization." 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702629 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702629 1083 #uuid "b071b47f-2819-58f0-9029-2235f9736418" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702630 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702660 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702630 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702630 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702630 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702660 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702630 1079 #uuid "b105b52f-d67c-5f04-9f64-f2c5d7a169ce" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702631 1075 #edb/ref 17592186702500 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702631 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702631 1077 "answer" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702631 1078 #edb/ref 17592186702500 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702631 1079 #uuid "b1f05870-a3a6-5713-92fb-c66be8566641" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702632 1058 #edb/ref 17592186702698 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702632 1062 #uuid "b3cbbb1b-ecf3-54f2-b1c8-04cd4cf168c6" 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702632 1063 #edb/ref 17592186702697 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702633 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048315 13194139540774 17592186502323 true] [17592186702633 10